// src/kernel/boot/witness/r31_respawn.pdx — R31.M2-1600 (#1600)
//
// The witness that proves the respawn arm works end-to-end. Runs immediately
// after witness_r31_spawn (from which it inherits the two boot-spawned
// tasks) and before init's ring-3 entry, so a broken respawn is diagnosed
// from the boot log rather than by init's silent inability to schedule
// against a dead server.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What #1600 changed and why this witness had to exist
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// R29's cascade re-armed a dead driver row and returned. Nothing recreated
// the userspace process behind the row, so the row's fresh incarnation held
// nothing that could bind. `#1600` closes that gap in three parts, wired in
// this order:
//
//   * boot_spawn.pdx records (image_base, image_len) per row at spawn time
//     and exposes boot_spawn_respawn(dying_pid, dying_gen) -> new_pid | 0.
//   * process_death.pdx gates on DRV_RESTART_OK, calls boot_spawn_respawn,
//     rebinds the row with driver_death_bind, and audits the respawn under
//     EV_RESTART outcome=4 ("respawned").
//   * this witness kills the R31.M2-spawned server, then observes the row
//     rebind to a new pid, the incarnation counter advance to 1, the row
//     state return to Init, and -- the load-bearing assertion -- the
//     service registry's own key move from OLD to NEW at the SAME row.
//
// The last assertion is the one that a "just rebind" implementation could
// pass while still being wrong. Appending the new (pid, gen) to the
// registry rather than rewriting the old row in place would satisfy every
// other check on this list; it would also exhaust the four-row registry
// after four restarts against one service, and every subsequent death
// would be silently dropped by boot_spawn_note_death because the ring's
// count no longer indexed the row it should. Assertion (e) below reads
// through boot_spawn_is_service to make the in-place rewrite visible: the
// OLD key must resolve to no row, and the NEW key must resolve to a row
// that exists.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Slot choice
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Slot 23 is deliberate and not decorative. The R29-family witnesses in
// this tree already claim slots 0, 8..11, 13..17, 21, 30 and a handful of
// others; slot 23 is the lowest single slot no other witness names, so
// this file makes exactly one row's worth of driver-table state its
// business and disturbs nothing. See design/drivers/witness-slot-plan.md
// for the running tally.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why runq_dequeue is load-bearing
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sys_exit_body sets the killed task's state to ZOMBIE and calls
// driver_death_notify. It does NOT dequeue the task from the runqueue,
// because in production every call site is a task exiting ITSELF and the
// caller performs the runq_dequeue-plus-pick-plus-switch tail (dispatch_exit
// in syscall/dispatch.pdx, and the four ring-3-fault dispatch tails in
// int/exceptions.pdx). Here we invoke sys_exit_body from kernel_main
// synchronously against a task that is NOT the current task, and no such
// tail runs. Without the dequeue below the ZOMBIE lives on the runqueue,
// sched_pick_next_r15 eventually picks it, sched_switch_r15 publishes it
// as _current_tcb, and the next sched_block seen by that thread of control
// trips the #663 guard with "SCHD ASSERT exp=1 got=3". The witness the
// prior implementation reproduced this on was boot_r17_shell_interactive's
// init.read -> tty_read -> sched_block chain; the panic is deterministic
// once the ZOMBIE has been picked. The one-line fix is the RIGHT one --
// dequeueing a ZOMBIE that was never the current task -- because it
// restores the invariant sched_pick_next_r15 trusts its callers to keep.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// R31.M2-1609 (#1609): endpoint resurrection witness (step F)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1600 rebound the DRIVER ROW to a fresh incarnation but left each
// endpoint bound to it stuck with the dead latch set — so the respawned
// process's first sys_ipc_recv returned -ECONNRESET on a channel whose
// server was alive. #1609 clears the latch in the respawn arm (via
// driver_restart_revive_endpoints inside process_death.pdx).
//
// This witness proves the fix end-to-end. Two new sub-tests bracket the
// kill: pre-kill (sub-test 5, the new one) allocates endpoint id 23 and
// binds it to driver slot 23, mimicking a sidecar with a hard-coded
// endpoint id (acpi_supervisor.pdx:151 pins endpoint_id=3, and every
// post-#1600 sidecar of the same shape carries the same contract);
// post-respawn (sub-test F, new stage 13) asserts endpoint_is_dead(23)
// returns 0 — the exact predicate sys_ipc_recv_body's ChannelDead gate
// consults, and the load-bearing observable this issue exists for.
//
// Why endpoint_is_dead rather than sys_ipc_recv_body directly: the gate
// IS the ChannelDead check. If endpoint_is_dead reports 0, the syscall
// would fall through the gate into user_bounce_recv — it would NOT
// return -ECONNRESET. Calling sys_ipc_recv_body itself would either
// block in sched_block on the empty pending slot (no message published,
// and publishing one would require a full frame_encode + valid user
// VAs), or short-circuit at endpoint_take_pending's empty sentinel and
// return via the block path — neither shape lets a witness assert what
// it needs to assert. The gate is the primitive and the assertion.
//
// Slot 23 is also the endpoint id: not a requirement, just a convention
// that makes the "endpoint 23 belongs to driver slot 23" association
// obvious in a log. Endpoint 23 is fresh at this point (r31_spawn
// claimed 1 and 2 via endpoint_alloc_at, no other witness claims 23),
// so endpoint_alloc_at(23) succeeds unconditionally.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The push at entry
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Same alignment argument r31_spawn.pdx's header states verbatim:
// boot_continue_after_ring3 runs its body at rsp %16 == 8, so a bare call
// into this function would present rsp %16 == 0 to the body and every
// nested-call alignment argument in this file would invert. The push
// restores parity; the pop and ret restore the caller.

module R31Respawn = structure {

  // witness_r31_respawn() -- proves boot_spawn_respawn + driver_death_bind
  // rebind a driver row to a fresh incarnation when the process holding
  // its previous incarnation dies.
  //
  // Reads _r31_server_task from R31Spawn (published by witness_r31_spawn's
  // sub-test 6). All other state is local .bss in this module. Effects and
  // capabilities widened @{boot} -> @{boot, cap, sched} for the same
  // reasons as the enclosing boot_continue_after_ring3 already declares:
  // {cap} from the transitive cap_owner_sweep_revoke inside the death
  // path, {sched} from runq_dequeue and the wake path.
  pub let witness_r31_respawn : () -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched} = fn () -> unsafe {
    effects: { mem, sysreg },
    capabilities: { cap, sched, boot },
    justification: "R31.M2-1600 (#1600): end-to-end respawn witness. Kills the server R31.M2-1590's witness_r31_spawn spawned (published to _r31_server_task at sub-test 6), then observes the row rebind. Slot 23 chosen so this witness names one row no other witness in the tree names; see slot header comment for the tally. Sequence: (0) precondition -- _r31_server_task != 0; (1) driver_table_register slot 23 with a synthetic name; (2) driver_lifecycle_transition Init -> Running so the row is a legal killable driver; (3) capture (old_pid, old_gen, incarnation-before) into .bss so the assertions read from a fixed source rather than from registers that must survive sys_exit_body; (4) driver_death_bind(23, old_pid) installs the correlation key so driver_death_notify resolves the pid to slot 23 rather than to no row; (5) sys_exit_body(server_task, 0) kills the task synchronously -- this triggers driver_death_notify's whole tail: cap_owner_sweep_revoke, boot_spawn_note_death (which emits R31 SPAWN CAP SWEEP OK count=1), driver_death_find_slot -> 23, driver_table_unbind_pid, EV_RESTART outcome=2, driver_restart_node -> DRV_RESTART_OK, boot_spawn_respawn -> new pid, driver_death_bind, EV_RESTART outcome=4; (6) runq_dequeue(server_task) is load-bearing -- see header for the SCHD ASSERT exp=1 got=3 discussion; (7) assertions (a)..(e) below, all keyed on the row and the registry rather than on the death path's own return; (8) emit R31 RESPAWN OK. Stage counter in .bss so a failure prints WHICH sub-test tripped via r31_respawn_fail_msg + line KV. Register discipline: entry push rbp restores the rsp %16 == 8 parity boot_continue_after_ring3 hands off, matching r31_spawn.pdx's idiom.",
    block: {
      push rbp;

      // ---- Sub-test 0: precondition -- the server task exists. ----
      //
      // witness_r31_spawn's sub-test 6 published this. A zero here means
      // that witness silently gave up before spawning the server, in which
      // case this witness has nothing to kill and reporting stage 0 makes
      // the ordering plain in the boot log.
      xor rax, rax;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;                              // stage = 0

      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_server_task];
      mov rax, [rcx];
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r31_respawn_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_server_task];
      mov [rcx], rax;                              // save the server slab

      // Capture server pid (u32 at slab+0).
      xor rcx, rcx;
      mov ecx, [rax + 0];
      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_old_pid];
      mov [rax], rcx;

      // Capture server generation via pid_gen_read(old_pid). Non-zero by
      // construction because the server has not been reaped yet.
      mov rdi, rcx;
      call pid_gen_read;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r31_respawn_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_old_gen];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      // ---- Sub-test 1: register driver slot 23. ----
      //
      // pid argument here is decorative -- the driver-row's pid field is
      // NOT the correlation key. Sub-test 4's driver_death_bind installs
      // the real key (pid, generation) in the row's pid_gen field. The
      // "42" mirrors the r29_driver_fsm witness's convention for the pid
      // field on a driver row that has no live process behind it.
      mov rax, 1;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;                                 // slot
      mov rsi, 42;                                 // decorative pid
      lea rdx, [rip + _r31_respawn_driver_name];
      xor rcx, rcx;                                // caps_manifest_offset
      call driver_table_register;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRIVER_TABLE_REGISTER_OK
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 2: drive the row Init -> Running. ----
      //
      // driver_restart_node's driver_restart_force_stop walks from any
      // state to Stopped via legal edges. Init IS a legal starting state
      // for that walk (Init -> Stopping -> Stopped is on the whitelist),
      // so this transition is not required for the cascade to work; it is
      // required for the ASSERTION that the row was re-armed FROM a
      // non-Init state back TO Init to be non-vacuous. If the row were
      // still Init at the moment of death, "state == Init after respawn"
      // would be true even if the re-arm had done nothing.
      mov rax, 2;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;
      mov rsi, 1;                                  // DRIVER_STATE_RUNNING
      call driver_lifecycle_transition;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRIVER_LIFECYCLE_OK
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 3: snapshot supervision incarnation-before. ----
      //
      // driver_sup_incarnation on a freshly-registered row that never
      // re-armed reads 0 (the .bss default). driver_table_rearm_incarnation
      // increments to 1 on the first re-arm, so assertion (c) below reads
      // exactly 1 post-death rather than a value that would collapse into
      // the pre-death reading.
      mov rax, 3;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;
      call driver_sup_incarnation;
      cmp rax, 0;
      jne r31_respawn_fail;                        // pre-death must be 0

      // ---- Sub-test 4: bind the row's death key to the server pid. ----
      //
      // driver_death_bind is the audited entry point that reads the
      // generation out of the scheduler for us. Its no-live-task refusal
      // is the same defence the initial bind uses; the server is live at
      // this moment so BIND_OK is required.
      mov rax, 4;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_old_pid];
      mov rsi, [rax];                              // pid
      mov rdi, 23;                                 // slot
      call driver_death_bind;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRV_DEATH_BIND_OK
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 12 (R31.M2-1609 #1609): bind endpoint 23 to slot 23. ----
      //
      // Deliberately runs BETWEEN the death-key bind (sub-test 4) and the
      // kill (sub-test 5) so the endpoint is present at the moment
      // driver_restart_reap_endpoints scans by driver_slot; without this,
      // the reap would find no endpoint to mark dead and step F (below)
      // would be vacuous. Stage tag is 12 rather than "4.5" to preserve
      // the existing 5..11 sub-test numbering unchanged — the header
      // notes the out-of-order tag on the two new stages.
      //
      // Endpoint id 23 mirrors the driver slot number by convention; the
      // "hard-coded endpoint id survives a crash" contract this issue
      // exists for (acpi_supervisor.pdx:151 pins id=3) does not require
      // the specific value, only that the SAME value is intact after
      // respawn. Endpoint 23 is fresh at boot time: r31_spawn claims 1
      // and 2 via endpoint_alloc_at and no other witness names 23.
      //
      // Incarnation argument 0 matches sub-test 3's snapshot: the row's
      // supervision incarnation reads 0 before any re-arm has occurred.
      // The endpoint's incarnation field is NOT rewritten by the revive
      // (see endpoint_dead_clear's justification), so it stays 0 across
      // the whole test — which is the correct reading for a client's
      // stale-cap defence: endpoint_attach_ok against the LIVE
      // incarnation (which reaches 1 post-respawn per assertion (c))
      // returns 0, forcing a client to re-cross sys_svc_lookup rather
      // than laundering authority.
      mov rax, 12;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;                                 // endpoint id
      call endpoint_alloc_at;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // EP_ALLOC_AT_OK
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      mov rdi, 23;                                 // id
      mov rsi, 23;                                 // driver_slot
      xor rdx, rdx;                                // incarnation = 0
      call endpoint_bind_driver;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // EP_BIND_OK
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- Sub-test 5: kill the server. ----
      //
      // sys_exit_body is the same primitive dispatch_exit and every ring-3
      // fault tail funnel through. Calling it against a task that is not
      // _current_tcb is unusual but well-defined: it operates on the slab
      // pointer passed in rdi, marks it ZOMBIE, sweeps its capabilities,
      // and hands its pid to driver_death_notify. Status 0 for the
      // "orderly synthetic kill" convention.
      mov rax, 5;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_server_task];
      mov rdi, [rax];                              // current = server slab
      xor rsi, rsi;                                // status = 0
      call sys_exit_body;

      // ---- Sub-test 6: runq_dequeue the freshly-ZOMBIE server. ----
      //
      // Load-bearing (see header for the SCHD ASSERT exp=1 got=3 story).
      // The dequeue is a two-store unlink on a task's own next/prev
      // pointers plus the head/tail patch -- runq_dequeue tolerates a
      // task that is already off the runqueue but the server is on it at
      // this point, so the store fires.
      mov rax, 6;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_server_task];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      call runq_dequeue;

      // ---- Sub-test 7 assertions (a): row rebound to non-zero pid. ----
      //
      // driver_table_read_pid returns the pid field of the row -- NOT the
      // death correlation key. driver_death_bind writes the correlation
      // key; that key is what a subsequent driver_death_notify would use
      // to resolve a fresh death. So assertion (a) reads the DEATH key
      // rather than the descriptive pid field, via driver_table_read_pid_gen:
      // if it is non-zero the bind fired, if it is zero the respawn arm
      // did not run through to driver_death_bind and the row is now
      // unbound.
      mov rax, 7;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;
      call driver_table_read_pid_gen;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r31_respawn_fail;

      // Capture the row's rebound pid via driver_table_read_pid; save it
      // for assertion (b) and for the boot_spawn_is_service check in
      // assertion (e). This is the pid the row is now keyed to, which the
      // respawn arm set to boot_spawn_respawn's return value.
      mov rdi, 23;
      call driver_table_read_pid;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r31_respawn_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_new_pid];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      // ---- (b) new pid != old pid ----
      //
      // task_new allocates a fresh pid via pid_alloc's dense-low-first
      // scan; the old pid slot is still occupied by the ZOMBIE server
      // (no parent to reap it -- parent_pid 0 by design in
      // boot_spawn_user_task), so the fresh pid cannot be the same
      // number. This is not the same claim as "the SCHEDULER can tell
      // them apart" -- that would be the generation counter -- but it
      // is the visible artifact of a real second task_new.
      mov rax, 8;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_old_pid];
      mov rcx, [rax];
      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_new_pid];
      mov rdx, [rax];
      cmp rdx, rcx;
      je  r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- (c) driver_sup_incarnation(23) == 1 ----
      //
      // driver_table_rearm_incarnation increments the incarnation counter
      // as its penultimate step. A row that had never re-armed reads 0;
      // one that re-armed once reads exactly 1. Any other reading here
      // means the cascade either failed silently before the re-arm or
      // ran it more than once, both of which are wrong in different
      // ways.
      mov rax, 9;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;
      call driver_sup_incarnation;
      cmp rax, 1;
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- (d) state == Init ----
      //
      // driver_table_rearm_incarnation zeroes the state byte as part of
      // its commit, taking the row from Stopped back to Init. This is
      // the observable that says "the row is ready for a fresh Running
      // transition", i.e. that the process the respawn arm created can
      // be bound and started against a real driver-row lifecycle rather
      // than against a corpse.
      mov rax, 10;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;
      call driver_lifecycle_get_state;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // DRIVER_STATE_INIT
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- (e) the in-place rewrite of the service registry. ----
      //
      // This is the assertion that a "just rebind" implementation would
      // pass while being wrong. If boot_spawn_respawn had APPENDED the
      // new row instead of rewriting the old one in place, the old
      // (pid, gen) would still be findable in the registry AND the new
      // one would too -- both halves of this check would pass, and the
      // registry would silently start leaking rows.
      //
      // So we assert BOTH halves:
      //   * OLD (pid, gen) must resolve to NO row (== 0 return).
      //   * NEW (pid, gen) must resolve to SOME row (!= 0 return).
      //
      // boot_spawn_is_service is the primitive for both readings; it
      // scans _boot_spawn_pids/_boot_spawn_gens directly, so the result
      // reflects what the registry now HOLDS rather than what the
      // respawn arm's return value CLAIMED it holds. See
      // boot_spawn.pdx §note_death for the "1-based row index or 0"
      // return convention.
      mov rax, 11;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_old_pid];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_old_gen];
      mov rsi, [rax];
      call boot_spawn_is_service;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // OLD must be gone
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      // NEW half: read the fresh generation with pid_gen_read on the
      // rebound pid, save it for the boot_spawn_is_service call.
      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_new_pid];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      call pid_gen_read;
      cmp rax, 0;
      je  r31_respawn_fail;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_new_gen];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_new_pid];
      mov rdi, [rax];
      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_new_gen];
      mov rsi, [rax];
      call boot_spawn_is_service;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // NEW must be present
      je  r31_respawn_fail;

      // ---- Emit the success marker for sub-tests 0..11 (the #1600 arm). ----
      //
      // No count KV: the interesting count -- the sweep count from
      // boot_spawn_note_death -- was already emitted by that path as
      // "R31 SPAWN CAP SWEEP OK count=1" when the server died above.
      // A second count here would be redundant and would risk confusing
      // the fingerprint grep for the sweep line, which is keyed on the
      // count.
      //
      // Emitted BEFORE step F so the fingerprint order "R31 RESPAWN OK"
      // -> "R31 RESPAWN IPC RECV OK" reflects the two claims separately:
      // the first says "the row rebound end-to-end (a..e passed)", the
      // second says "the endpoint the respawned server serves is not
      // stuck marked ChannelDead". A step-F failure leaves the first
      // marker in place, so a debugger can pin the regression to #1609
      // rather than to the whole respawn chain.
      mov rdi, 3;                                  // LEVEL_INFO
      lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
      lea rdx, [rip + r31_respawn_ok_msg];
      call klog_s1;

      // ---- Sub-test 13 (R31.M2-1609 #1609): step F, endpoint resurrection. ----
      //
      // The load-bearing #1609 assertion: after the respawn arm ran,
      // endpoint 23 (bound at sub-test 12 pre-kill, latched dead by
      // driver_restart_reap_endpoints inside driver_restart_node,
      // then supposedly revived by driver_restart_revive_endpoints in
      // the respawn arm) must NOT read as dead.
      //
      // endpoint_is_dead is exactly the predicate sys_ipc_recv_body's
      // ChannelDead gate consults: if it returns 0, the syscall would
      // fall through the gate into user_bounce_recv and NOT return
      // -ECONNRESET. Calling sys_ipc_recv_body directly here would
      // either block in sched_block on the empty pending slot (nothing
      // was published; publishing a valid message would require a full
      // frame_encode + valid user VAs) or short-circuit through
      // endpoint_take_pending's empty sentinel into the same block
      // path — neither shape lets a witness assert what step F needs
      // to assert. The gate is the primitive and the assertion.
      //
      // A non-zero return here means the revive did not clear the
      // dead latch on endpoint 23 — the exact #1609 regression this
      // witness exists to catch.
      mov rax, 13;
      lea rcx, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov [rcx], rax;

      mov rdi, 23;                                 // endpoint id
      call endpoint_is_dead;
      cmp rax, 0;                                  // dead latch must be CLEAR
      jne r31_respawn_fail;

      mov rdi, 3;                                  // LEVEL_INFO
      lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
      lea rdx, [rip + r31_respawn_ipc_recv_ok_msg];
      call klog_s1;
      jmp r31_respawn_done;

      r31_respawn_fail:
      // Same shape as r31_spawn.pdx's fail path: WHICH sub-test tripped,
      // as a decimal KV so a stage number can be read from the golden.
      // Deliberately non-fatal: the boot continues into ring-3 so a
      // broken respawn is diagnosed from init's wire output rather than
      // by a silent hang at the marker's absence.
      lea rax, [rip + _r31_respawn_stage];
      mov r8, [rax];
      mov rdi, 1;                                  // LEVEL_ERROR
      lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
      lea rdx, [rip + r31_respawn_fail_msg];
      lea rcx, [rip + k_line];
      call klog_s1_d1;

      r31_respawn_done:
      pop rbp;
      ret
    }
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Witness storage
  //
  // Every value that outlives a nested call site is in .bss rather than in
  // a register, matching the discipline r31_spawn.pdx documents in its own
  // storage block: sys_exit_body's call chain does not preserve callee-save
  // for us across module boundaries in a way any compiler check enforces,
  // so a witness that parked one of these in r12/r13/r14 would be one
  // future edit to a downstream module away from silently reading garbage.
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

  // The 16-byte name driver_table_register copies verbatim into row 23.
  // "respawn-witness" is 15 characters plus one NUL for a 16-byte payload;
  // driver_table_register reads exactly two qwords starting at name_ptr,
  // so the padding is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
  pub let _r31_respawn_driver_name : [u8; 16] = "respawn-witness\0"

  // Sub-test index, mirrored in the failure path as a decimal KV so the
  // golden can pin WHICH assertion tripped rather than just knowing one
  // did.
  pub let mut _r31_respawn_stage : u64 = 0

  // The R31.M2-spawned server this witness kills. Copied at sub-test 0
  // from R31Spawn._r31_server_task so the slab pointer survives the
  // sys_exit_body call in a fixed .bss slot.
  pub let mut _r31_respawn_server_task : u64 = 0

  // The (pid, generation) pair of the server BEFORE death, captured for
  // assertion (e)'s "OLD must be gone" reading through
  // boot_spawn_is_service. Reading via pid_gen_read AFTER the death would
  // resolve the wrong incarnation (the recycled slot -- pid_free does not
  // reset _pid_gen), which is why these are captured pre-kill.
  pub let mut _r31_respawn_old_pid : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _r31_respawn_old_gen : u64 = 0

  // The (pid, generation) pair of the RESPAWNED server, read out of the
  // driver row AFTER the death path returned. new_pid comes from
  // driver_table_read_pid (which the respawn arm set via driver_death_bind);
  // new_gen comes from pid_gen_read(new_pid) at assertion (e)'s NEW half.
  pub let mut _r31_respawn_new_pid : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _r31_respawn_new_gen : u64 = 0

  // Fingerprints. Matching the "MARKER key=value" shape r31_spawn.pdx uses
  // so the same golden-line parser handles both: no trailing newline,
  // klog_emit_core appends the KV list and the terminator itself.
  pub let r31_respawn_ok_msg   : [u8; 15] = "R31 RESPAWN OK\0"
  pub let r31_respawn_fail_msg : [u8; 17] = "R31 RESPAWN FAIL\0"

  // R31.M2-1609 (#1609): step F's success marker. Emitted iff the
  // endpoint bound to slot 23 pre-kill was successfully revived by the
  // respawn arm's driver_restart_revive_endpoints. Golden ordering pins
  // this to appear immediately after r31_respawn_ok_msg — see
  // tests/r31/expected-spawn-pair.txt.
  //
  // 23 chars + NUL terminator = 24 bytes; matches the length shape of
  // r31_respawn_ok_msg / r31_respawn_fail_msg above (character count + 1).
  pub let r31_respawn_ipc_recv_ok_msg : [u8; 24] = "R31 RESPAWN IPC RECV OK\0"
}
